 stricter restrictions  than elsewhere  what should we expect to hear tomorrow  well the prime minister's going to make  an announcement tomorrow so i don't want  to preempt what he  intends to say but in addition to the  basic  simple rules that apply to the whole  country we're  designing a framework for those places  for where the virus is  very strong and there will be some  rules which apply consistently but then  also  we're in a very productive conversations  with local leaders  local directors of public health to ask  them if there are any further measures  that they would support which will help  to control the virus in their local  communities  so we want the strongest possible  working relationship now  between central government and local  government because the virus although  it's spreading concerningly across the  whole of  the uk it is very concentrated in some  places and so it's right that we take  action  in those communities and design the  measures  in conjunction with people who know  those places best you say the about the  importance of rules being applied  consistently  consistently so on what basis will areas  move between  different levels will it be based on  case numbers the r rate hospital  emissions  will there be a publicly available  system  well i think you'll have to wait to see  what the prime minister says on that  regard but  we've taken into account historically a  range of different factors  the number of cases per hundred thousand  is significant but there are also other  important factors  uh which we can we can use as well  including hospitalizations  and the nature of those cases whether uh  they are across the whole of the  community  or whether they're more contained in  particular settings  and what exactly should people expect if  they are in  the highest tier could there be a ban on  mixing  with other households even outside  well i can't say that today because you  have to wait for tomorrow  what we have said is that accompanying  those measures  will be a very comprehensive package of  support the chancellor has already set  out  the support for people where business is  asked to close as a result of these  additional measures that the government  will step in and pay two-thirds  of their wages he's also been clear that  those businesses will be able to apply  for grants of up to three thousand  pounds to help them cover their rent and  other expenses in addition to the  measures that he's already put in place  like  the vat cup for hospitality the business  rates holiday  uh for retail and hospitality and in  addition businesses  should be expecting what businesses  should be expecting to be ordered  to close pubs restaurants  well i'm not going to say that sophie  because you'll have to wait for tomorrow  it wouldn't be right for me to announce  on  the program what the prime minister will  say to parliament you say you see  you say with respect you say it wouldn't  be right for you to say that but  we've been given briefings for a long  time in the newspapers  every time you pick up the press you can  see more leaks local leaders are saying  that they're finding out  what's going on by reading these leaks i  mean we're not stupid they're obviously  coming from  government tens of millions of people  will be feeling that you know  sick fear of uncertainty not sure what's  coming you've got an  opportunity here to give people the  clarity that they need that businesses  need  and you're just ducking the questions  no that's not right the the prime  minister is going to set out  the way forward in the house of commons  that is the right way  to proceed the chancellor has already  set out very clearly  the very strong financial support that  we're putting in place  i appreciate that people working in  certain sectors and business owners  will be feeling very concerned but he  set out i think a fair and generous  package  to help to support them and we've spent  the weekend working with those local  leaders it was not correct what you  heard from  jonathan reynolds that hasn't been good  communication i've spent the whole  weekend talking to leaders  from merseyside from greater manchester  from other parts of the country  we're trying to work very closely with  mayors with council leaders  and chief executives to design these  measures with them that does take time  that's because we want to have good  communication between national  and local government before we announce  how we're going to take this forwards  you've been talking about the measures  put in place by the chancellor the  announcement that  the government will step in to pay  two-thirds of people's wages if  businesses are ordered to close  will there be any further financial  support for  areas uh under these postcode lockdowns  well i think that is a very significant  intervention it's building on the  winter economy plan that the chancellor  had set out two weeks ago that was very  well received it was supported  by the tuc and the cbi as you say it  ensures that those workers in businesses  that are asked to close  by law are then able to receive  two-thirds of their unearned  wages we're also providing those  business grants to the small and  medium-sized businesses  that will need them this comes on top of  the other measures many of those workers  will have been on the furlough scheme  beforehand and the latest measures we're  introducing do compare favorably  internationally it's broadly the same as  what's happening in france  and spain more generous than what's  happening in italy and germany  so i think this is specifically about  whether there'll be any more  financial support or is that it  well we can't do everything you know we  are in a deep recession  the national debt is rising i think most  reasonable people  would see the chancellor's put in place  an unprecedented package of support  this is a good safety net for those  individuals but we understand  t