onal  tracing infrastructure and also to have  a greater say  in track and trace as well talking about  test and trace  we currently spend more on test and  trace than we do in nurseries  and universities but the latest stats  show that the system is still  only contacting 69 of people  identified i mean is this strategy  really working  well as i say it has moved forward  enormously  over the course of the summer from a  very low base back in march now to where  we are with 310  000 tests a day we've contacted i think  over 700  000 people and helped them to  self-isolate protect  themselves and others from the virus so  it isn't  it is a constantly evolving and  improving infrastructure what we're  doing now as i say is also  bringing in the the local level as well  so we can use  all the resources of local councils  effectively as weather as well as other  resources we've offered for example  the armed forces to communities like  merseyside  and greater manchester and they can play  a role in doing this  our testing infrastructure is improving  but it does  compare favorably with other countries  we're now testing about the same if not  more  than most other developed countries  we're bringing more people  into the system we're opening up new  labs all the time and of course we'll  keep on  uh ramping up that infrastructure over  the course of the autumn and the winter  it's difficult to kind of escape the  view that as infections continue to rise  as people like jonathan van tam said  that we're at a tipping point  that the system is effectively being  overwhelmed  and you know the government's strategy  has been what boris johnson turned  whack-a-mole to  target with these local lockdowns areas  where the virus is  flaring up but with so many people tens  of millions of people  under these local lockdowns it's hard to  see that they're local anymore and we  know the moles now winning  well although the number of cases rising  rapidly across the country  there are still huge variations you know  if you go to north norfolk i think  the latest statistics showed that the  number of cases is around 19.  if you go to manchester it's well over  500.  so it is right that we pursue a  localized approach that must be  the way forward because none of us want  to see a return to blanket national  measures that would be the alternative  and that's not one that we want that  localized when one and three of us are  under national lockdown though does it  well as i say there isn't an alternative  to doing that because you don't want to  see  blanket measures across the country we  want to stick to the simple rules  elsewhere like washing your hands  uh wearing a mask staying apart on the  rule of six and have more localized  targeted measures in the places that  really need them  what we're doing now is going further  and offering local communities  their local leadership the opportunity  to come forward further with further  measures that they think will be  particularly  effective in those places and that's the  kind  of conversations we've been having over  the course of this weekend  okay now if you look at the places that  have seen these  big increases in the virus an awful lot  of them  are places where there are two  universities a big student populations  thinking places like nottingham  liverpool manchester when we look back  uh on the handling of the crisis with  hindsight  was it a big mistake to allow university  students to return  no the prime minister chose to  prioritize education  and employment wherever we can i think  that was the right choice you know these  are difficult judgments  but it's a valid judgment that it was  right to help young people to get back  into  face-to-face full-time education  wherever possible the return of schools  was the right thing to do 99 percent of  schools  are open and i think getting young  people back to university was also  the right decision universities have put  in place measures  uh whether that's trying to shield count  campuses where possible  using bubbles uh substituting  face-to-face education where they have  to  uh with remote learning so they have  used the measures that are available to  them  but you know there are no easy choices  here and we made a balanced judgment  that it was right to get people back  into education  rather than allowing the life chances  and opportunities of young people  to be put on hold any longer  and just finally um after the interview  i'm going to show a film  that i made this week i go to colchester  united football club league two club  like many really struggling to stay  afloat without the money they get from  live fans  going there why is it safe for people to  go to the pub  go to the cinema but not at socially  distance  outside football games  well you know these are difficult  choices and there are trade-offs with  each decision that we make  but the culture secretary oliver dowden  has been working extremely closely  with the football league and the  premiership to work through  uh the way in which we could safely get  fans back to watching sport we would all  love  to see that and if we can we will do  but we've got to make difficult choices  at the moment and we've chosen to  prioritize  education and employment and things like  that so  we will keep on making those difficult  decisions as far as we can but  the cultural sector is working very  closely with the sports sector  okay robert jamrick thank you very much  for being on the program today thank you

